WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:09.620
Watch one microtubule grow from a seed in a dish of purified tubulin.

2
00:00:10.120 --> 00:00:15.780
It lengthens slowly for minutes, then shrinks many times faster than it grew.

3
00:00:16.620 --> 00:00:18.480
Sometimes it starts growing again.

4
00:00:19.300 --> 00:00:22.740
A crystal in its solution does not behave like this.

5
00:00:23.300 --> 00:00:25.640
Why would a polymer be built to fail?

6
00:00:26.550 --> 00:00:32.110
Plot the length of one plus end against time, in a trace made up for teaching.

7
00:00:33.050 --> 00:00:37.630
It grows for three minutes, from five to nine and a half micrometres.

8
00:00:38.510 --> 00:00:40.910
That is one and a half micrometres per minute.

9
00:00:41.910 --> 00:00:46.910
Then it loses six micrometres in twelve seconds, and the line plunges.

10
00:00:47.550 --> 00:00:50.970
That is thirty micrometres per minute, twenty times faster.

11
00:00:51.783 --> 00:00:53.543
Why does an end fail?

12
00:00:54.623 --> 00:00:59.483
Each tubulin dimer joins the end with GTP on its β-tubulin.

13
00:01:00.283 --> 00:01:06.183
The GTP is hydrolysed soon after, once the dimer sits inside the lattice behind the

14
00:01:06.183 --> 00:01:11.903
end. GDP-tubulin is held in the straight lattice only by its neighbours.

15
00:01:12.983 --> 00:01:17.663
The end stays intact while a cap of dimers not yet hydrolysed remains.

16
00:01:18.717 --> 00:01:23.577
If addition slows and hydrolysis catches up, the cap is lost.

17
00:01:24.457 --> 00:01:29.757
The end comes apart quickly, with protofilaments peeling outward from the tip, and

18
00:01:29.757 --> 00:01:35.097
it shortens fast. That switch from growth to shortening is a catastrophe.

19
00:01:35.977 --> 00:01:37.557
The switch back is a rescue.

20
00:01:38.637 --> 00:01:44.077
Each end switches this way on its own, and the behaviour is called dynamic instability.

21
00:01:44.750 --> 00:01:47.230
The cap idea has been tested in experiments.

22
00:01:47.230 --> 00:01:54.090
In 2020, a team used recombinant human tubulin carrying mutations

23
00:01:54.090 --> 00:01:55.610
that slow hydrolysis.

24
00:01:56.330 --> 00:01:57.710
The caps grew longer.

25
00:01:58.730 --> 00:02:04.210
Earlier work on pig brain tubulin, free of other proteins, found that more tubulin

26
00:02:04.210 --> 00:02:07.010
made catastrophes rarer at both ends.

27
00:02:07.670 --> 00:02:10.390
That fits a larger cap at faster growth.

28
00:02:11.283 --> 00:02:17.763
Binding drives assembly, and hydrolysis comes afterwards and makes the old lattice

29
00:02:17.763 --> 00:02:23.543
less stable. A cap of dimers not yet hydrolysed holds the end together.

30
00:02:24.603 --> 00:02:28.163
Exactly what commits an end to shortening is still argued.

31
00:02:29.343 --> 00:02:35.563
Keep the rule. Lose the cap, and the end shortens rapidly, many times faster than it grew.
